    "username": "godofprompt",
    "nickname": "God of Prompt",
    "text": "Check your Cursor install today.\n\nFor about 11 hours on October 29, Cursor's extension marketplace served tampered copies of the Claude Code extension, versions 2.1.312 and 2.1.313, reportedly reaching up to 5,000 users. Anthropic says it did not build them, and that its official channels are unaffected.\n\nThe code added an attacker's SSH key, tried to open a reverse tunnel, and installed a startup job that retries every 60 seconds. Turning on SSH usually needs admin rights, so that step often failed. Cursor says the key and the startup job still landed.\n\nthe default that costs you: updating the extension and assuming you're clean. Cursor says uninstalling may not be enough.\n\nwhat to run on a Mac or Linux machine:\n\ngrep svc-telemetry ~/.ssh/authorized_keys\nls ~/.config/.smt ~/.config/.smtk\n\nOn Windows, search your authorized_keys file for svc-telemetry and look for a scheduled task named SysMon.\n\nIf anything turns up:\n\n1. Isolate the machine.\n2. Run Cursor's remediation script, or rebuild the machine.\n3. Rotate credentials and sessions from a different, trusted device.\n\nthe check: the script exits non-zero if anything remains.\n\nIf your team uses Cursor, they need this in plain language today. I use a prompt that rates the severity and turns an incident into a short bulletin with the steps.\n\nCursor has since rotated its keys and rebuilt the marketplace.",
    "quoted_tweet": null,
    "url": "https://x.com/godofprompt/status/2116666582294560956",
    "time": "2026-11-01 07:00",
    "created_at": "Sat Oct 31 23:00:13 +0000 2026",
    "id_str": "2116666582294560956",
    "is_retweet": false
  },
